+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSocket connections that the project operates itself, with no realtime vendor in the path. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed around that route. The change store keeps event revisions replayable from any revision a browser names and publishes committed changes to delivery reliably. Delivery owns socket sessions: accepting connections, fanning committed revisions out to the sessions subscribed to an event, and shedding or refusing connections when capacity is reached. Browsers subscribe over a socket, detect a dropped connection, reconnect, and catch up from their last known revision rather than assuming the board is unchanged. Operating this route means owning connection capacity and reconnect behavior, which is accepted in exchange for fast updates and no vendor coupling.
 
